import jax
from jax import lax

def f(x):
  @jax.jit
  def g():
    return x, lax.while_loop(lambda _: x < 0., lambda x: x, x)
  y, z = g()
  return y + lax.stop_gradient(z)

jax.grad(f)(3.)
That code raises an error when the jax.jit is present, but succeeds if we remove it.
The issues are:

while_loop is linearizable but not transposable;
in this example there's a while_loop included in the jaxpr to be transposed, though it's 'dead code' and doesn't affect the value of the tangent (because of the stop_gradient);
that dead while_loop is included in the jaxpr to be transposed because the "DCE" we get for free (via the data dependence mechanism built into partial evaluation) can be foiled by inner calls, in the sense that we only know the while_loop is dead in the tangent program after we apply stop_gradient, so we don't know it's dead when we form the linear jaxpr for g (and hence it's not pruned from the jaxpr for g).

A solution would be to run DCE on a jaxpr before transposing it.
(This came up in practice in a surprising way: when setting FLAX_PROFILE=1, a transposability error arose, since that attaches a bunch of jax.named_calls to Flax modules, which had a similar effect to adding jax.jit above. That particular trigger via named_call will go away with @sharadmv 's new named_call implementation, but as the above repro shows this can be an issue just with jax.jit.)

I'm writing a model in jax/flax and I use pytorch utils.data.DataLoader for multiprocessed data loading. The dataset functions however do not use pytorch tensors but numpy and jax, because some of the code I use for my model is also relevant for data preprocessing (various 3d geometry routines). This works with just CPU but when I run it on a GPU server I run into
2021-12-09 14:23:32.808066: F external/org_tensorflow/tensorflow/stream_executor/cuda/cuda_driver.cc:153] Failed setting context: CUDA_ERROR_NOT_INITIALIZED: initialization error

which I believe to be because of spawning multiple CUDA-using processes. If I duplicate the geometry-related functions to use np instead of jnp, everything works fine. Ideally, I would like to have a way to instruct jax to execute all instructions in some context on CPU and avoid the code duplication. I envision it as something like
class Dataset:
   ...
   def _getitem_impl(self, index):
      # uses np and jnp code intermixed
      return data_item

   def __getitem__(self, index):
      with jax.default_device_placement('cpu'):
         return self._getitem_impl(index)
I saw that there are other issues asking about similar features, however in my case jax.device_put won't really cut it because of the intermixing of jax and numpy code, which would force me to add device_put at every API boundary. It is also not something that can be solved with global flags because I still want the model in the main process to run on GPU. Would it be difficult to add such a feature?

This concerns an unusual use of host_callback, inside a custom primitive whose XLA lowering rule uses xla.lower_fun.
This uses private JAX core APIs and your mileage may vary.
This usage is not working properly when the function passed to lower_fun includes host callback primitives.
    class Prim(jax.core.Primitive):
      def __init__(self):
        super(Prim, self).__init__("prim")
        self.multiple_results = True

        def _abstract(*flat_avals, **params):
          return pe.abstract_eval_fun(self.impl, *flat_avals, **params)

        self.def_abstract_eval(_abstract)

        def _xla(c, *xla_args, **params):
          translation = xla.lower_fun(self.impl, multiple_results=True,
                                      backend="cpu")
          return translation(c, *xla_args, **params)

        xla.translations[self] = _xla

      def impl(self, *args):
        x, = args
        ret = hcb.id_print(x * 3., output_stream=testing_stream)
        return ret,

    @jax.jit
    def fn(x):
      return Prim().bind(x)[0]

    res = fn(jnp.asarray(1.))

This crashes with TypeError: _outside_call_translation_rule() missing 1 required keyword-only argument: 'has_token'

Hi, I'm interested in using custom_ivjp to wrap invertible functions but am finding that its not possible to JIT calls to jax.vjp using custom_ivjp.  I get the error message NotImplementedError: XLA translation rule for primitive 'custom_ivjp' not found.  I'm wondering if there are any plans to implement this soon?  Below is a rough outline of how I am hoping to use custom_ivjp:
import jax
from jax import random
import jax.numpy as jnp

def invertible_affine(x, params, inverse=False):
  A, b = params["A"], params["b"]
  log_det = jnp.linalg.slogdet(A)[1]
  if inverse:
    z = jax.vmap(jnp.linalg.solve, in_axes=(None, 0))(A, x - b)
  else:
    z = jnp.einsum("ij,bj->bi", A, x) + b
  return z, log_det*jnp.ones(x.shape[:1])

class ivjpWrapper():
  def __init__(self, flow):
    self.flow = flow
    self.directions = {}
    # Wrap the forward and inverse pass with a custom_ivjp
    self.make_apply_direction(True)
    self.make_apply_direction(False)

  def make_apply_direction(self, inverse):
    def apply_fun(x, params):
      return self.flow(x, params, inverse=inverse)
    fwd = jax.custom_ivjp(apply_fun)

    @fwd.defivjp
    def defivjp(inputs, outputs, output_cotangents):
      (x, params), (z, log_det) = inputs, outputs
      x_reconstr, log_det_reconstr = self.flow(z, params, inverse=not inverse)
      _, vjp = jax.vjp(apply_fun, x_reconstr, params)
      return (x_reconstr, params), vjp(output_cotangents)

    self.directions[inverse] = jax.invertible(fwd)

  def __call__(self, x, params, inverse=False):
    fun = self.directions[inverse]
    return fun(x, params)

if __name__ == "__main__":
  rng_key = random.PRNGKey(1)
  k1, k2, k3 = random.split(rng_key, 3)

  x_dim = 4
  x = random.normal(k1, (64, x_dim))
  A = random.normal(k2, (x_dim, x_dim))
  b = random.normal(k3, (x_dim,))
  params = dict(A=A, b=b)

  flow = invertible_affine
  wrapped_flow = ivjpWrapper(flow)

  jax.jit(wrapped_flow)(x, params)
  print("this runs")

  def test(x, params):
    (z, log_det), vjp = jax.vjp(wrapped_flow, x, params)
    return vjp((z, log_det))

  # This runs
  test(x, params)
  print("here!")

  # This fails
  jax.jit(test)(x, params)
  print("here?")
Also am I using custom_ivjp correctly?  This code seems to give the correct answers but I'm not 100% sure because there isn't any documentation.
